---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Sessions 15&ndash;16: Receive &amp; Translate the Brief</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">The Arcade Cabinet Project &mdash; Milestone 1 of 5</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>The Arcade Cabinet Project</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Sessions 15&ndash;28</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Four Weeks Ago, You Practiced This</h2>
              <p>Session 1: translating a vague ask into User, Need, Constraints, Success Criteria.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Today, the ask is real, and so is everything that follows it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>The Arc</h2>
              <table class="table table-dark table-sm">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Sessions</th><th>Milestone</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>15&ndash;16</td><td>Receive &amp; Translate the Brief</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>17&ndash;20</td><td>Concept, Material &amp; CAD</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>21&ndash;22</td><td>CAM Programming</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>23&ndash;26</td><td>Fabrication &amp; Build</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>27&ndash;28</td><td>Present Back &amp; Reflect</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>You're a Company. You Have a Client.</h2>
              <p>
                Your client is either a real business-class team or your
                instructor, playing that role &mdash; the process is
                identical either way.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>No Two Teams Get the Same Brief</h2>
              <p>
                This isn't a shared spec. Your cabinet's direction is
                genuinely your own team's, based on your own client.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>What a Brief Usually Includes</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment">Who the cabinet is for, or where it'll live</li>
                <li class="fragment">A theme, mood, or vibe</li>
                <li class="fragment">Any must-have features</li>
                <li class="fragment">A rough budget or timeline, if one exists</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Same Skill as Session 1</h2>
              <p>
                Draft the four parts. Mark what's explicit, implied, or
                missing. Write real clarifying questions &mdash; specific
                enough your client can actually answer them.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Ask Before You Sketch</h2>
              <p>
                It's tempting to start designing the moment you have a
                theme. Don't &mdash; Session 1's whole lesson was resisting
                exactly that.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">A real client's answers may be messier than a practice one.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                They might not know their own budget precisely, or change
                their mind after a question. That's real collaboration, not
                a flaw in the exercise.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">Your written requirements are what Milestone 2 builds from.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                A vague or incomplete translation here becomes a genuinely
                harder problem two weeks from now.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Up</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">Concept, Material &amp; CAD (Sessions 17&ndash;20).</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Sketching real concepts and modeling a real cabinet, from the
                requirements you write this week.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: Receive Your Brief</h3>
          <p>
            Read the brief your instructor provides (from a real business
            partner team, or an instructor-played client). Don't start
            sketching yet.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Draft the Four Parts</h3>
          <p>For each part, mark whether it's explicit, implied, or missing:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><b>User</b> &mdash; who is this cabinet actually for?</li>
            <li><b>Need</b> &mdash; what's the real need behind the request?</li>
            <li><b>Constraints</b> &mdash; budget, space, timeline, required features?</li>
            <li><b>Success Criteria</b> &mdash; how will your client know it worked?</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Write Clarifying Questions</h3>
          <p>
            Write at least 3 questions for your client, specific enough they
            can actually answer them without needing to think like an
            engineer.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 4: Get Answers and Finalize Requirements</h3>
          <p>
            Once you have answers (live from a partner team, or from your
            instructor), write a final, one-page requirements summary
            covering all four parts. This is what Milestone 2 starts from.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you move on, confirm your requirements summary:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Names a specific user, not a vague group</li>
            <li>States a real need, not a solution in disguise</li>
            <li>Lists concrete constraints, even if some are "none given, confirmed with client"</li>
            <li>States success criteria you could actually check at the end</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              How did translating a real brief feel different from Session
              1's practice bus stop scenario?
            </li>
            <li>
              Did any of your client's answers surprise you, or change your
              read on the request?
            </li>
            <li>
              Looking at your finalized requirements, which part &mdash;
              User, Need, Constraints, or Success Criteria &mdash; do you
              expect to most shape your concept sketches next session?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your team's four-part draft, your clarifying questions, your finalized requirements summary, and your individual reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Milestone Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
